1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,129
In January, one pavan of gold crossed ₹1,31,000.

2
00:00:05,129 --> 00:00:08,010
Today it is only about ₹92,000.

3
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:11,122
That is almost ₹40,000 less on every pavan.

4
00:00:11,122 --> 00:00:19,075
This is big news for families who keep gold at home, those waiting to buy for a wedding, and everyone with a gold loan.

5
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:21,610
Why is the gold price falling like this?

6
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:22,532
Will it fall further?

7
00:00:22,532 --> 00:00:25,068
And what should gold loan borrowers watch out for now?

8
00:00:25,068 --> 00:00:27,500
Let's go through it all, simply.

9
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:29,238
First, the numbers.

10
00:00:29,238 --> 00:00:38,274
On world markets, gold hit a record of $5,589 an ounce on 28 January.

11
00:00:38,274 --> 00:00:43,316
On 20 November it fell to about $3,520.

12
00:00:43,316 --> 00:00:46,066
That is a fall of about 36%.

13
00:00:46,066 --> 00:00:51,829
Last week alone it fell about 9.5%, the worst week in eight months.

14
00:00:51,829 --> 00:00:57,722
In Kerala, one pavan cost ₹1,01,840 on 1 November.

15
00:00:57,722 --> 00:01:02,502
On 21 November it was ₹91,720.

16
00:01:02,502 --> 00:01:06,207
More than ₹10,000 gone in this one month alone.

17
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:11,059
The biggest reason is America's central bank, the Federal Reserve, or the Fed.

18
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:13,488
Inflation in the US is still high.

19
00:01:13,488 --> 00:01:17,794
To control it, markets expect the Fed to raise interest rates.

20
00:01:17,794 --> 00:01:25,633
In the Fed meeting minutes released on 18 November, almost all members said further rate rises would likely be needed.

21
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:29,467
Markets now see a December rate hike as almost certain.

22
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:32,079
Why should gold fall when interest rates rise?

23
00:01:32,079 --> 00:01:33,362
Here's a simple example.

24
00:01:33,362 --> 00:01:36,358
Imagine you have one lakh rupees.

25
00:01:36,358 --> 00:01:38,925
Put it in a bank and you earn interest every year.

26
00:01:38,925 --> 00:01:41,432
But gold in a locker pays no interest.

27
00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:45,894
The higher interest rates go, the more you give up by holding gold.

28
00:01:45,894 --> 00:01:51,091
In the US, 10-year government bonds now pay about 5%.

29
00:01:51,091 --> 00:01:55,853
So big investors sell gold and move money into bonds.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,274
The second reason is a strong dollar.

31
00:01:58,274 --> 00:02:02,237
Gold is priced in dollars all over the world.

32
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:08,018
When the dollar gets stronger, gold costs more for buyers in other countries, so demand falls.

33
00:02:08,018 --> 00:02:12,176
The dollar is now near a 19-month high.

34
00:02:12,176 --> 00:02:15,294
The third reason is the AI crash in the stock market.

35
00:02:15,294 --> 00:02:23,260
As shares like Nvidia plunged, many investors were forced to sell their gold to cover losses.

36
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,453
Now, back home.

37
00:02:25,453 --> 00:02:29,494
The gold price in Kerala depends on two things.

38
00:02:29,494 --> 00:02:33,004
The world price in dollars, and the rupee-dollar exchange rate.

39
00:02:33,004 --> 00:02:35,587
When the world price falls, it falls here too.

40
00:02:35,587 --> 00:02:38,170
If the rupee gets stronger, the price here falls even more.

41
00:02:38,170 --> 00:02:44,100
In Kerala, the pavan price is down about 30% from January's record.

42
00:02:44,233 --> 00:02:46,000
This isn't the first time gold has crashed.

43
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,081
In 2013, gold fell about 28% in a single year.

44
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:59,252
It took until 2020, nine years, to beat the 2011 record again.

45
00:02:59,252 --> 00:03:02,018
After that, gold went on to new highs.

46
00:03:02,018 --> 00:03:05,971
So for people holding gold for the long term, patience matters.

47
00:03:05,971 --> 00:03:10,053
But people with short-term gold loans don't get that much time.

48
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:16,251
For families waiting to buy gold for a wedding or other needs, this fall is a relief.

49
00:03:16,251 --> 00:03:20,106
In January, 10 pavan cost over ₹13 lakh.

50
00:03:20,106 --> 00:03:23,072
Today it's about ₹9.17 lakh.

51
00:03:23,072 --> 00:03:27,165
But no one can know in advance which day will be the lowest.

52
00:03:27,165 --> 00:03:32,564
So many people buy in stages over a few months instead of all on one day.

53
00:03:32,564 --> 00:03:36,660
Don't forget to add making charges and GST to your sums.

54
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,520
Now the most important part: gold loans.

55
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:44,329
When prices were high, many families pledged gold and took loans.

56
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:52,237
RBI data showed bank gold loans grew 125% in a single year to November 2025.

57
00:03:52,237 --> 00:04:00,019
The loan is set as a percentage of the gold's value. This is called LTV, loan-to-value.

58
00:04:00,019 --> 00:04:06,472
Under RBI rules from 1 April, loans up to ₹2.5 lakh can be at most 85%.

59
00:04:06,472 --> 00:04:11,580
₹2.5 to 5 lakh: 80%. Above ₹5 lakh: 75%.

60
00:04:11,733 --> 00:04:13,170
Let's take an example.

61
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:16,660
Say someone pledged 5 pavan in January.

62
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:22,681
Then, one pavan was ₹1,30,000, so the gold was worth ₹6.5 lakh.

63
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:24,939
They took a loan of ₹4.5 lakh.

64
00:04:24,939 --> 00:04:30,755
Today the same 5 pavan is worth only about ₹4.59 lakh.

65
00:04:30,755 --> 00:04:38,076
So the loan is about 98% of the gold's value, and more once interest is added.

66
00:04:38,076 --> 00:04:48,913
To get back to the allowed 80%, they may need to repay about ₹83,000, or pledge more than one extra pavan.

67
00:04:49,067 --> 00:04:51,381
This is called a margin call.

68
00:04:51,381 --> 00:04:56,985
The lender may ask you to pay part of the loan, or to pledge more gold.

69
00:04:56,985 --> 00:05:04,599
If you ignore the notice and miss repayments, the lender can auction the gold after giving notice.

70
00:05:04,599 --> 00:05:11,665
Under the new rules, the auction reserve price cannot be below 90% of the gold's current value.

71
00:05:11,665 --> 00:05:17,447
Any money left after clearing the loan must be returned to you within 7 working days.

72
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,210
So, if you have a gold loan, here's what to do now.

73
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:27,205
One: compare your loan plus interest so far with today's gold value.

74
00:05:27,205 --> 00:05:32,813
Two: talk to your bank or lender before any notice arrives.

75
00:05:32,813 --> 00:05:35,133
Three: never ignore a notice.

76
00:05:35,133 --> 00:05:41,193
Four: deal only with RBI-regulated banks and lenders.

77
00:05:41,193 --> 00:05:44,780
This is general information, not financial advice.

78
00:05:44,933 --> 00:05:47,688
So what could happen in the next few months?

79
00:05:47,688 --> 00:05:52,201
Nobody can say for sure, but here's what to watch.

80
00:05:52,201 --> 00:05:57,301
Goldman Sachs expects the Fed to raise rates in December and again in March.

81
00:05:57,301 --> 00:06:00,407
While rates are rising, pressure on gold may continue.

82
00:06:00,407 --> 00:06:05,096
But many big banks expect prices to recover next year.

83
00:06:05,096 --> 00:06:11,836
The median forecast in a Reuters poll is $4,650 an ounce for 2027.

84
00:06:11,836 --> 00:06:16,643
Central banks around the world are also still buying gold.

85
00:06:16,643 --> 00:06:19,873
But remember, these forecasts have changed many times.

86
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:26,409
In short: Fed rate hikes, a strong dollar and the stock market crash are pulling gold down.

87
00:06:26,409 --> 00:06:29,167
If you have a gold loan, check your numbers today.

88
00:06:29,167 --> 00:06:36,585
Use the free gold calculator at calcsouq.com to work out what your gold is worth today.

89
00:06:36,585 --> 00:06:43,144
If this helped, share it with family and friends who have gold loans.

90
00:06:43,144 --> 00:06:45,773
And don't forget to subscribe.
